WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.080
Good morning Hank. It's Tuesday,

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:03.780
today's video is like Alice's Doc McStuffins Lego

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:06.000
It comes to you in only one part.

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:07.400
Part 1: The news.

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:10.300
I am of course referring Hank, to the El Chapo news

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:13.180
So a couple of weeks ago I got a text from my mother-in-law that said

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:15.780
"You need to read the El Chapo story in the Washington Post"

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:21.320
El Chapo, for those unfamiliar with his work, is the currently imprisoned alleged leader of the Sinaloa Drug Cartel,

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:25.300
and according to the Washington Post article in question, the world's most notorious drug lord.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:28.260
He's known for his ruthlessness and daring prison escapes

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:32.540
and from everything I can gather he's like not the kind of person you want as an enemy

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:36.960
so I'm gonna tread pretty likely here Hank, because I am nothing if not a coward.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:41.600
Right, so anyway I start reading the article, which details some concerns that El Chapo's lawyer has about

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:43.480
the conditions of El Chapo's imprisonment.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:47.580
It's reported, for instance, that El Chapo is only allowed to watch one hour of television per day

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:50.340
and also that during his sixty-five days in the Juarez Prison,

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:53.760
he has only been allowed two intimate visits with a concubine.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:58.160
The article also asserts that during his imprisonment, El Chapo has read two books:

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:02.040
the Spanish novel El Final del Ave Fenix by Martin Querol,

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:04.140
...and John Green's high school love story Paper Towns.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:08.120
Now first off, I'd argue that Paper Towns isn't so much a love story as a learning

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:11.240
that romanticizing someone isn't the same thing as loving them story,

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:14.100
but yeah, apparently El Chapo has read one of my books.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:18.220
which, y'know, is a little weird. I mean, I guess Paper Towns is about a larger than life character

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:22.980
who enjoys well-plotted escapes and has a bit of an empathy deficit, so maybe he found it relatable?

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:27.520
I have so many questions! Did he like the book? How was the Spanish translation? Did he ship Ben and Radar?

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:31.320
Did he emerge from the reading experience imagining Santa more complexly?

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:35.920
But the biggest question I have is like, HOW? How did a book I wrote eight years ago

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:38.520
end up in a prison in Juarez, Mexico?

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:42.840
Which led to an even larger question, I started to wonder how, like, ANYONE ever found my books.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:45.400
You might be thinking, "Well, everyone else was reading them", or whatever

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:49.420
But in fact, that is not true! While my books have been far more widely read than I ever could've imagined,

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:52.240
more than 97% of Americans haven't read one.

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:57.560
So my readers are still in a relatively small club, Hank - it's just like, y'know, you, a few other people, EL CHAPO

00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:00.740
In fact, at this point, reading ANY book is almost a counter-cultural activity

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:05.100
like a recent survey found that almost 30% of American adults read no books in the last year.

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:08.140
So, nerdfighters, no worries if you haven't read one of my books, but if you have,

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:11.460
I was wondering if you can share with me in comments how you came to read it.

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:13.560
Or I'd love to hear the story of any book that found its way to you

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:15.760
through strange and unexpected circumstances.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:19.200
And yes, I am hoping to use these answers for inspiration in the coming months

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:20.420
as I work on my new story

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:23.760
Speaking of which, I should go back to that - Hank, I will see you on Friday.

